CAT = "cmaComm"; SEG = "COM"
Q = [
("A patient newly told she has diabetes says, \"The lab must have mixed up my blood.\" Which defense mechanism is she using?",
 "Denial", ["Projection", "Sublimation", "Regression"],
 "Denial is refusing to accept a painful reality. It often protects a patient for a short time after bad news; staff stay patient and repeat information later."),
("A patient who is angry at himself for missing doses says, \"You people are angry with me for being late on my pills.\" Which defense mechanism is this?",
 "Projection", ["Displacement", "Repression", "Compensation"],
 "Projection is attributing one's own feelings to someone else. Displacement would be taking the anger out on a safer target."),
("After the provider gives him bad news, a patient snaps at the receptionist about parking. Which defense mechanism is this?",
 "Displacement", ["Rationalization", "Projection", "Denial"],
 "Displacement moves feelings from the real source (the news) to a safer target (the receptionist). Recognizing it helps staff respond calmly rather than take it personally."),
("A potty-trained 4-year-old starts wetting the bed after a new baby arrives. Which defense mechanism is this?",
 "Regression", ["Repression", "Sublimation", "Intellectualization"],
 "Regression is returning to behavior from an earlier stage of development under stress. It is common in young children and usually passes."),
("A patient with a terminal diagnosis says, \"Why is this happening to me? It isn't fair!\" Which stage of grief does this show?",
 "Anger", ["Denial", "Depression", "Acceptance"],
 "In the Kübler-Ross model, anger often shows as \"Why me?\" and frustration aimed at others. Staff accept the feeling without arguing."),
("In Maslow's hierarchy, which needs must be met first?",
 "Physiological needs", ["Esteem needs", "Belonging needs", "Self-actualization needs"],
 "Physiological needs such as air, food, water and sleep form the base of the hierarchy. A hungry or in-pain patient is not ready to learn new material."),
("During teaching, a patient sits with arms crossed and turned away. What does this nonverbal cue most likely mean?",
 "She feels closed off", ["She fully agrees with the plan", "She is ready to ask questions", "She wants more detail now"],
 "Crossed arms and turning away often signal discomfort or resistance. The medical assistant checks in gently rather than pressing on."),
("Which behavior shows the patient that the medical assistant is listening?",
 "Eye contact and nodding", ["Typing while the patient talks", "Finishing the patient's sentences", "Glancing at the clock"],
 "Attentive posture, appropriate eye contact and brief nods show interest. Interrupting or multitasking tells the patient the listener is distracted."),
("A patient quietly admits he cannot read well. How should the medical assistant give his discharge instructions?",
 "Explain aloud and use pictures", ["Hand him the printed sheet", "Tell him to ask a neighbor", "Send the sheet to his portal only"],
 "Verbal explanation with pictures and a demonstration, checked by having him explain it back, works without embarrassing him."),
("A patient with an intellectual disability comes with a caregiver. How should the medical assistant communicate?",
 "Speak to the patient simply", ["Talk only with the caregiver", "Use medical terms to be exact", "Raise the voice and speak slowly"],
 "The patient is addressed directly with short, concrete sentences, involving the caregiver as needed. Ignoring the patient is disrespectful."),
("A deaf patient who uses sign language books an appointment. Under the ADA, what is the practice generally required to do?",
 "Provide one at no cost", ["Charge the patient for one", "Ask her to bring a relative", "Use only written notes"],
 "The ADA requires effective communication, which for a sign language user is often a qualified interpreter, at no charge to the patient. Family members are not a substitute."),
("A 15-year-old comes for a sports physical with a parent. How should the interview be handled?",
 "Offer time alone with the teen", ["Ask the parent every question", "Have the parent fill in forms", "Let the teen answer in writing"],
 "Giving adolescents time without a parent lets them answer sensitive questions honestly, within the confidentiality rules of the state and practice."),
("A medical assistant assumes an 80-year-old patient cannot use the patient portal and never offers it. What is this?",
 "A stereotype based on age", ["Good time management by staff", "Respect for privacy", "Appropriate triage"],
 "Assuming ability from age is a stereotype. Every patient is offered the same services and helped as needed."),
("A patient avoids direct eye contact while answering questions. How should the medical assistant interpret this?",
 "It may be cultural", ["She is clearly lying", "She is not listening", "She is refusing care"],
 "In some cultures avoiding eye contact shows respect. The medical assistant does not judge the patient by one cue and asks if anything is unclear."),
("A patient makes repeated sexual comments to a medical assistant during visits. What should she do?",
 "Set a limit and tell a supervisor", ["Laugh it off to keep the peace", "Refuse to room him ever again", "Post about it online to warn others"],
 "Clear professional boundaries protect staff and patients. The behavior is stated as unacceptable, reported to a supervisor and documented."),
("A patient becomes loud and threatening at the front desk. Which de-escalation step is best?",
 "Speak calmly, keep a safe distance", ["Raise your voice to take back control", "Block the door to stop him", "Touch his arm to calm him"],
 "A calm, low voice, open posture and space between you and the patient lower tension and keep an exit clear. Touch or a raised voice can escalate the situation."),
("A patient is referred to a nephrologist. Which body system does this specialist treat?",
 "Kidneys", ["Nerves", "Skin and hair", "Heart"],
 "Nephrologists treat kidney disease. Neurologists treat nerves, dermatologists skin, and cardiologists the heart."),
("In the role of patient navigator, what does a medical assistant do?",
 "Guide patients through care", ["Diagnose new conditions", "Write prescriptions", "Approve insurance claims for them"],
 "A navigator helps patients schedule tests, find resources and follow the care plan across providers. Diagnosis and prescribing stay with the provider."),
("Which incoming call is usually put straight through to the provider?",
 "Another physician calling", ["A sales representative", "A patient's routine refill request", "A billing question"],
 "Calls from other physicians are usually connected promptly or as office policy says. Routine refills and billing questions are handled by staff or taken as messages."),
("The medical assistant needs to put a caller on hold. What is the correct technique?",
 "Ask first, then wait for a reply", ["Press hold without saying anything", "Say 'hold' and press at once", "Leave the line open on the desk"],
 "Asking and waiting gives an emergency caller the chance to say so. Leaving the line open lets the caller overhear other patients' information."),
]
